helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3154352	26			0	uk	\<emph\>Data\</emph\> represents the reference to the values to be counted.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3148402	27			0	uk	\<emph\>Classes\</emph\> represents the array of the limit values.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN10D71				0	uk	You can find a general introduction to Array functions at the top of this page.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148981	28			0	uk	Example				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3155904	219			0	uk	In the following table, column A lists unsorted measurement values. Column B contains the upper limit you entered for the classes into which you want to divide the data in column A. According to the limit entered in B1, the FREQUENCY function returns the number of measured values less than or equal to 5. As the limit in B2 is 10, the FREQUENCY function returns the second result as the number of measured values that are greater than 5 and less than or equal to 10. The text you entered in B6, ">25", is only for reference purposes.				20220827 09:49:47
