helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060111.xhp	0	help	par_id3147402	60			0	uk	WEEKS(StartDate; EndDate; Type)				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060111.xhp	0	help	par_id3151387	61			0	uk	\<emph\>StartDate\</emph\> is the first date				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060111.xhp	0	help	par_id3146324	62			0	uk	\<emph\>EndDate\</emph\> is the second date				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060111.xhp	0	help	par_id3166467	63			0	uk	\<emph\>Type\</emph\> calculates the type of difference. The possible values are 0 (interval) and 1 (in numbers of weeks).				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060111.xhp	0	help	bm_id3145237				0	uk	\<bookmark_value\>WEEKSINYEAR function\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>number of weeks;in a specific year\</bookmark_value\>				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060111.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145237	64			0	uk	WEEKSINYEAR				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060111.xhp	0	help	par_id3147410	65			0	uk	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_DAI_FUNC_WEEKSINYEAR\"\>Calculates the number of weeks of the year in which the date entered occurs.\</ahelp\> The number of weeks is defined as follows: a week that spans two years is added to the year in which most days of that week occur.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060111.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149719	66			0	uk	Syntax				20220827 09:49:47
