helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\text2columns.xhp	0	help	par_id655232				0	uk	\<variable id=\"text2columns\"\>Opens the Text to Columns dialog, where you enter settings to expand the contents of selected cells to multiple cells. \</variable\>				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\text2columns.xhp	0	help	hd_id9599597				0	uk	To expand cell contents to multiple cells				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\text2columns.xhp	0	help	par_id2021546				0	uk	You can expand cells that contain comma separated values (CSV) into multiple cells in the same row. 				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\text2columns.xhp	0	help	par_id2623981				0	uk	For example, cell A1 contains the comma separated values \<item type=\"literal\"\>1,2,3,4\</item\>, and cell A2 contains the text \<item type=\"literal\"\>A,B,C,D\</item\>. 				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\text2columns.xhp	0	help	par_id7242042				0	uk	Select the cell or cells that you want to expand.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\text2columns.xhp	0	help	par_id6999420				0	uk	Choose \<emph\>Data - Text to Columns\</emph\>.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\text2columns.xhp	0	help	par_id6334116				0	uk	You see the Text to Columns dialog.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\text2columns.xhp	0	help	par_id9276406				0	uk	Select the separator options. The preview shows how the current cell contents will be transformed into multiple cells.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\text2columns.xhp	0	help	par_id8523819				0	uk	You can select a fixed width and then click the ruler on the preview to set cell breakup positions.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\text2columns.xhp	0	help	par_id1517380				0	uk	You can select or enter separator characters to define the positions of breaking points. The separator characters are removed from the resulting cell contents.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\text2columns.xhp	0	help	par_id7110812				0	uk	In the example, you select the comma as a delimiter character. Cells A1 and A2 will be expanded to four columns each. A1 contains 1, B1 contains 2, and so on.				20220827 09:49:47
