helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3154508	28			0	uk	The entries 2, 4, 6 and eight in the Value 1-4 fields are to be counted.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3158000	29			0	uk	            \<item type=\"input\"\>=COUNTA(2;4;6;"eight")\</item\> = 4. The count of values is therefore 4.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	bm_id3150267				0	uk	\<bookmark_value\>B function\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>probabilities of samples with binomial distribution\</bookmark_value\>				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150267	31			0	uk	B				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3156061	32			0	uk	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_B\"\>Returns the probability of a sample with binomial distribution.\</ahelp\>				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150659	33			0	uk	Syntax				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3148392	34			0	uk	B(Trials; SP; T1; T2)				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3149002	35			0	uk	            \<emph\>Trials\</emph\> is the number of independent trials.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3148875	36			0	uk	            \<emph\>SP\</emph\> is the probability of success on each trial.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3145352	37			0	uk	            \<emph\>T1\</emph\> defines the lower limit for the number of trials.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3149538	38			0	uk	            \<emph\>T2\</emph\> (optional) defines the upper limit for the number of trials.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148768	39			0	uk	Example				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3154633	40			0	uk	What is the probability with ten throws of the dice, that a six will come up exactly twice? The probability of a six (or any other number) is 1/6. The following formula combines these factors:				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3149393	41			0	uk	            \<item type=\"input\"\>=B(10;1/6;2)\</item\> returns a probability of 29%.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	bm_id3158416				0	uk	\<bookmark_value\>RSQ function\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>determination coefficients\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>regression analysis\</bookmark_value\>				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3158416	43			0	uk	RSQ				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3154949	44			0	uk	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_BESTIMMTHEITSMASS\"\>Returns the square of the Pearson correlation coefficient based on the given values.\</ahelp\> RSQ (also called determination coefficient) is a measure for the accuracy of an adjustment and can be used to produce a regression analysis.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3152820	45			0	uk	Syntax				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3155822	46			0	uk	RSQ(DataY; DataX)				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3150470	47			0	uk	            \<emph\>DataY\</emph\> is an array or range of data points.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3153181	48			0	uk	            \<emph\>DataX\</emph\> is an array or range of data points.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156258	49			0	uk	Example				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3155991	50			0	uk	            \<item type=\"input\"\>=RSQ(A1:A20;B1:B20)\</item\> calculates the determination coefficient for both data sets in columns A and B.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	bm_id3145620				0	uk	\<bookmark_value\>BETAINV function\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>cumulative probability density function;inverse of\</bookmark_value\>				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145620	52			0	uk	BETAINV				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3149825	53			0	uk	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_BETAINV\"\>Returns the inverse of the cumulative beta probability density function.\</ahelp\>				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3152479	54			0	uk	Syntax				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3156300	55			0	uk	BETAINV(Number; Alpha; Beta; Start; End)				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3149266	56			0	uk	            \<emph\>Number\</emph\> is the value between \<emph\>Start\</emph\> and \<emph\>End\</emph\> at which to evaluate the function.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3149710	57			0	uk	            \<emph\>Alpha\</emph\> is a parameter to the distribution.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3156306	58			0	uk	            \<emph\>Beta\</emph\> is a parameter to the distribution.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3150960	59			0	uk	            \<emph\>Start\</emph\> (optional) is the lower bound for \<emph\>Number\</emph\>.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3151268	60			0	uk	            \<emph\>End\</emph\> (optional) is the upper bound for \<emph\>Number\</emph\>.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_idN109DF				0	uk	            \<embedvar href=\"text/scalc/00/00000004.xhp#optional\"/\>         				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147077	61			0	uk	Example				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3146859	62			0	uk	            \<item type=\"input\"\>=BETAINV(0.5;5;10)\</item\> returns the value 0.33.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	bm_id3156096				0	uk	\<bookmark_value\>BETADIST function\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>cumulative probability density function;calculating\</bookmark_value\>				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156096	64			0	uk	BETADIST				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3150880	65			0	uk	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_BETAVERT\"\>Returns the beta function.\</ahelp\>				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150762	66			0	uk	Syntax				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3147571	67			0	uk	BETADIST(Number; Alpha; Beta; Start; End; Cumulative)				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3156317	68			0	uk	            \<emph\>Number\</emph\> is the value between \<emph\>Start\</emph\> and \<emph\>End\</emph\> at which to evaluate the function.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3156107	69			0	uk	            \<emph\>Alpha\</emph\> is a parameter to the distribution.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3153619	70			0	uk	            \<emph\>Beta\</emph\> is a parameter to the distribution.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3150254	71			0	uk	            \<emph\>Start\</emph\> (optional) is the lower bound for \<emph\>Number\</emph\>.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3149138	72			0	uk	            \<emph\>End\</emph\> (optional) is the upper bound for \<emph\>Number\</emph\>.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id012020091254453				0	uk	            \<emph\>Cumulative\</emph\> (optional) can be 0 or False to calculate the probability density function. It can be any other value or True or omitted to calculate the cumulative distribution function.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_idN10AB3				0	uk	            \<embedvar href=\"text/scalc/00/00000004.xhp#optional\"/\>         				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145649	73			0	uk	Example				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3156118	74			0	uk	            \<item type=\"input\"\>=BETADIST(0.75;3;4)\</item\> returns the value 0.96				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	bm_id3143228				0	uk	\<bookmark_value\>BINOMDIST function\</bookmark_value\>				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3143228	76			0	uk	BINOMDIST				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3146897	77			0	uk	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_BINOMVERT\"\>Returns the individual term binomial distribution probability.\</ahelp\>				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149289	78			0	uk	Syntax				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3156009	79			0	uk	BINOMDIST(X; Trials; SP; C)				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3154304	80			0	uk	            \<emph\>X\</emph\> is the number of successes in a set of trials.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3147492	81			0	uk	            \<emph\>Trials\</emph\> is the number of independent trials.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3146085	82			0	uk	            \<emph\>SP\</emph\> is the probability of success on each trial.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3149760	83			0	uk	            \<emph\>C\</emph\> = 0 calculates the probability of a single event and \<emph\>C\</emph\> = 1 calculates the cumulative probability.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3151171	84			0	uk	Example				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3145666	85			0	uk	            \<item type=\"input\"\>=BINOMDIST(A1;12;0.5;0)\</item\> shows (if the values \<item type=\"input\"\>0\</item\> to \<item type=\"input\"\>12\</item\> are entered in A1) the probabilities for 12 flips of a coin that \<emph\>Heads\</emph\> will come up exactly the number of times entered in A1.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3150120	86			0	uk	            \<item type=\"input\"\>=BINOMDIST(A1;12;0.5;1)\</item\> shows the cumulative probabilities for the same series. For example, if A1 = \<item type=\"input\"\>4\</item\>, the cumulative probability of the series is 0, 1, 2, 3 or 4 times \<emph\>Heads\</emph\> (non-exclusive OR).				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	bm_id0119200902432928				0	uk	\<bookmark_value\>CHISQINV function\</bookmark_value\>				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id0119200902421451				0	uk	CHISQINV				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id0119200902421449				0	uk	\<ahelp hid=\".\"\>Returns the inverse of CHISQDIST.\</ahelp\>				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id0119200902475241				0	uk	Syntax				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id0119200902475286				0	uk	            \<emph\>Probability\</emph\> is the probability value for which the inverse of the chi-square distribution is to be calculated.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id0119200902475282				0	uk	            \<emph\>Degrees Of Freedom\</emph\> is the degrees of freedom for the chi-square function.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	bm_id3148835				0	uk	\<bookmark_value\>CHIINV function\</bookmark_value\>				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148835	88			0	uk	CHIINV				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3149906	89			0	uk	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_CHIINV\"\>Returns the inverse of the one-tailed probability of the chi-squared distribution.\</ahelp\>				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3159157	90			0	uk	Syntax				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3150504	91			0	uk	CHIINV(Number; DegreesFreedom)				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3154898	92			0	uk	            \<emph\>Number\</emph\> is the value of the error probability.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3154294	93			0	uk	            \<emph\>DegreesFreedom\</emph\> is the degrees of freedom of the experiment.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154208	94			0	uk	Example				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3150777	130			0	uk	A die is thrown 1020 times. The numbers on the die 1 through 6 come up 195, 151, 148, 189, 183 and 154 times (observation values). The hypothesis that the die is not fixed is to be tested.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3153062	131			0	uk	The Chi square distribution of the random sample is determined by the formula given above. Since the expected value for a given number on the die for n throws is n times 1/6, thus 1020/6 = 170, the formula returns a Chi square value of 13.27.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3148806	132			0	uk	If the (observed) Chi square is greater than or equal to the (theoretical) Chi square CHIINV, the hypothesis will be discarded, since the deviation between theory and experiment is too great. If the observed Chi square is less that CHIINV, the hypothesis is confirmed with the indicated probability of error.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3149763	95			0	uk	            \<item type=\"input\"\>=CHIINV(0.05;5)\</item\> returns 11.07.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3159142	133			0	uk	            \<item type=\"input\"\>=CHIINV(0.02;5)\</item\> returns 13.39.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3158401	134			0	uk	If the probability of error is 5%, the die is not true. If the probability of error is 2%, there is no reason to believe it is fixed.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	bm_id3154260				0	uk	\<bookmark_value\>CHITEST function\</bookmark_value\>				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154260	97			0	uk	CHITEST				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3151052	98			0	uk	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_CHITEST\"\>Returns the probability of a deviance from a random distribution of two test series based on the chi-squared test for independence.\</ahelp\> CHITEST returns the chi-squared distribution of the data.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3148925	135			0	uk	The probability determined by CHITEST can also be determined with CHIDIST, in which case the Chi square of the random sample must then be passed as a parameter instead of the data row.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154280	99			0	uk	Syntax				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3149162	100			0	uk	CHITEST(DataB; DataE)				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3158421	101			0	uk	            \<emph\>DataB\</emph\> is the array of the observations.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3166453	102			0	uk	            \<emph\>DataE\</emph\> is the range of the expected values.				20220827 09:49:47
