helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060106.xhp	0	help	bm_id3147356				0	uk	\<bookmark_value\>GCD function\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>greatest common divisor\</bookmark_value\>				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060106.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147356	237			0	uk	GCD				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060106.xhp	0	help	par_id3152465	238			0	uk	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_GGT\"\>Returns the greatest common divisor of two or more integers.\</ahelp\>				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060106.xhp	0	help	par_id2769249				0	uk	The greatest common divisor is the positive largest integer which will divide, without remainder, each of the given integers.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060106.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150643	239			0	uk	Syntax				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060106.xhp	0	help	par_id3154524	240			0	uk	GCD(Integer1; Integer2; ...; Integer30)				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060106.xhp	0	help	par_id3149340	241			0	uk	\<emph\>Integer1 To 30\</emph\> are up to 30 integers whose greatest common divisor is to be calculated.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060106.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147317	242			0	uk	Example				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060106.xhp	0	help	par_id3151285	243			0	uk	\<item type=\"input\"\>=GCD(16;32;24) \</item\>gives the result 8, because 8 is the largest number that can divide 16, 24 and 32 without a remainder.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060106.xhp	0	help	par_id1604663				0	uk	\<item type=\"input\"\>=GCD(B1:B3)\</item\> where cells B1, B2, B3 contain \<item type=\"input\"\>9\</item\>, \<item type=\"input\"\>12\</item\>, \<item type=\"input\"\>9\</item\> gives 3.				20220827 09:49:47
