helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3156304	99			0	uk	            \<emph\>Alpha\</emph\> is the percentage of the marginal data that will not be taken into consideration.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	hd_id3151180	100			0	uk	Example				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3156130	101			0	uk	            \<item type=\"input\"\>=TRIMMEAN(A1:A50; 0.1)\</item\> calculates the mean value of numbers in A1:A50, without taking into consideration the 5 percent of the values representing the highest values and the 5 percent of the values representing the lowest ones. The percentage numbers refer to the amount of the untrimmed mean value, not to the number of summands.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	bm_id3153216				0	uk	\<bookmark_value\>ZTEST function\</bookmark_value\>				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153216	103			0	uk	ZTEST				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3150758	104			0	uk	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_GTEST\"\>Calculates the probability of observing a z-statistic greater than the one computed based on a sample.\</ahelp\>				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150872	105			0	uk	Syntax				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3153274	106			0	uk	ZTEST(Data; mu; Sigma)				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3156109	107			0	uk	            \<emph\>Data\</emph\> is the given sample, drawn from a normally distributed population.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3149977	108			0	uk	            \<emph\>mu\</emph\> is the known mean of the population.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3154740	109			0	uk	            \<emph\>Sigma\</emph\> (optional) is the known standard deviation of the population. If omitted, the standard deviation of the given sample is used.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id0305200911372999				0	uk	See also the \<link href=\"https://wiki.openoffice.org/wiki/Documentation/How_Tos/Calc:_ZTEST_function\"\>Wiki page\</link\>.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	bm_id3153623				0	uk	\<bookmark_value\>HARMEAN function\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>means;harmonic\</bookmark_value\>				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153623	113			0	uk	HARMEAN				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3155102	114			0	uk	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_HARMITTEL\"\>Returns the harmonic mean of a data set.\</ahelp\>				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	hd_id3146900	115			0	uk	Syntax				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3149287	116			0	uk	HARMEAN(Number1; Number2; ...Number30)				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3154303	117			0	uk	            \<emph\>Number1,Number2,...Number30\</emph\> are up to 30 values or ranges, that can be used to calculate the harmonic mean.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	hd_id3159179	118			0	uk	Example				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3146093	120			0	uk	            \<item type=\"input\"\>=HARMEAN(23;46;69)\</item\> = 37.64. The harmonic mean of this random sample is thus 37.64				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	bm_id3152801				0	uk	\<bookmark_value\>HYPGEOMDIST function\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>sampling without replacement\</bookmark_value\>				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	hd_id3152801	122			0	uk	HYPGEOMDIST				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3159341	123			0	uk	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_HYPGEOMVERT\"\>Returns the hypergeometric distribution.\</ahelp\>				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154697	124			0	uk	Syntax				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3155388	125			0	uk	HYPGEOMDIST(X; NSample; Successes; NPopulation)				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3154933	126			0	uk	            \<emph\>X\</emph\> is the number of results achieved in the random sample.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3153106	127			0	uk	            \<emph\>NSample\</emph\> is the size of the random sample.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3146992	128			0	uk	            \<emph\>Successes\</emph\> is the number of possible results in the total population.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3148826	129			0	uk	            \<emph\>NPopulation \</emph\>is the size of the total population.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150529	130			0	uk	Example				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3154904	131			0	uk	            \<item type=\"input\"\>=HYPGEOMDIST(2;2;90;100)\</item\> yields 0.81. If 90 out of 100 pieces of buttered toast fall from the table and hit the floor with the buttered side first, then if 2 pieces of buttered toast are dropped from the table, the probability is 81%, that both will strike buttered side first.				20220827 09:49:47
