helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\05\empty_cells.xhp	0	help	par_id2476577				0	uk	=ISBLANK(VLOOKUP(1;A1:C1;3)) => FALSE (C1)				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\05\empty_cells.xhp	0	help	par_id4217047				0	uk	Note that Microsoft Excel behaves different and always returns a number as the result of a reference to an empty cell or a formula cell with the result of an empty cell. For example:				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\05\empty_cells.xhp	0	help	par_id2629474				0	uk	A1: <empty>				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\05\empty_cells.xhp	0	help	par_id8069704				0	uk	B1: =A1 => displays 0, but is just a reference to an empty cell				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\05\empty_cells.xhp	0	help	par_id4524674				0	uk	=ISNUMBER(A1) => FALSE				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\05\empty_cells.xhp	0	help	par_id4396801				0	uk	=ISTEXT(A1) => FALSE				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\05\empty_cells.xhp	0	help	par_id5293740				0	uk	=A1=0 => TRUE				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\05\empty_cells.xhp	0	help	par_id7623828				0	uk	=A1="" => TRUE				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\05\empty_cells.xhp	0	help	par_id2861720				0	uk	=ISNUMBER(B1) => FALSE (MS-Excel: TRUE)				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\05\empty_cells.xhp	0	help	par_id9604480				0	uk	=ISTEXT(B1) => FALSE				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\05\empty_cells.xhp	0	help	par_id2298959				0	uk	=B1=0 => TRUE				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\05\empty_cells.xhp	0	help	par_id4653767				0	uk	=B1="" => TRUE (MS-Excel: FALSE)				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\05\empty_cells.xhp	0	help	par_id8801538				0	uk	C1: =VLOOKUP(...) with empty cell result => displays empty (MS-Excel: displays 0)				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\05\empty_cells.xhp	0	help	par_id6746421				0	uk	=ISNUMBER(VLOOKUP(...)) => FALSE				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\05\empty_cells.xhp	0	help	par_id4876247				0	uk	=ISTEXT(VLOOKUP(...)) => FALSE				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\05\empty_cells.xhp	0	help	par_id7458723				0	uk	=ISNUMBER(C1) => FALSE (MS-Excel: TRUE)				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\05\empty_cells.xhp	0	help	par_id2753379				0	uk	=ISTEXT(C1) => FALSE				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\goalseek.xhp	0	help	tit				0	uk	Applying Goal Seek				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\goalseek.xhp	0	help	bm_id3145068				0	uk	\<bookmark_value\>goal seeking;example\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>equations in goal seek\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>calculating;variables in equations\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>variables;calculating equations\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>examples;goal seek\</bookmark_value\>				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\goalseek.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145068	22			0	uk	\<variable id=\"goalseek\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/goalseek.xhp\" name=\"Applying Goal Seek\"\>Applying Goal Seek\</link\>\</variable\>				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\goalseek.xhp	0	help	par_id3145171	2			0	uk	With the help of Goal Seek you can calculate a value that, as part of a formula, leads to the result you specify for the formula. You thus define the formula with several fixed values and one variable value and the result of the formula.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\goalseek.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153966	14			0	uk	Goal Seek Example				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\goalseek.xhp	0	help	par_id3150871	4			0	uk	To calculate annual interest (I), create a table with the values for the capital (C), number of years (n), and interest rate (i). The formula is:				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\goalseek.xhp	0	help	par_id3152596	5			0	uk	I = C * n* i				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\goalseek.xhp	0	help	par_id3155335	15			0	uk	Let us assume that the interest rate \<item type=\"literal\"\>i\</item\> of 7.5% and the number of years \<item type=\"literal\"\>n\</item\> (1) will remain constant. However, you want to know how much the investment capital \<item type=\"literal\"\>C\</item\> would have to be modified in order to attain a particular return \<item type=\"literal\"\>I\</item\>. For this example, calculate how much capital \<item type=\"literal\"\>C\</item\> would be required if you want an annual return of $15,000.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\goalseek.xhp	0	help	par_id3155960	6			0	uk	Enter each of the values for Capital \<item type=\"literal\"\>C\</item\> (an arbitrary value like \<item type=\"literal\"\>$100,000\</item\>), number of years \<item type=\"literal\"\>n \</item\>(\<item type=\"literal\"\>1\</item\>), and interest rate \<item type=\"literal\"\>i\</item\> (\<item type=\"literal\"\>7.5%\</item\>) in one cell each. Enter the formula to calculate the interest \<item type=\"literal\"\>I\</item\> in another cell. Instead of \<item type=\"literal\"\>C\</item\>, \<item type=\"literal\"\>n\</item\>, and \<item type=\"literal\"\>i\</item\> use the \<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/relativ_absolut_ref.xhp\"\>reference to the cell\</link\> with the corresponding value.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\goalseek.xhp	0	help	par_id3147001	16			0	uk	Place the cursor in the cell containing the interest \<item type=\"literal\"\>I\</item\>, and choose \<emph\>Tools - Goal Seek\</emph\>. The \<emph\>Goal Seek\</emph\> dialog appears.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\goalseek.xhp	0	help	par_id3150088	17			0	uk	The correct cell is already entered in the field \<emph\>Formula Cell\</emph\>.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\goalseek.xhp	0	help	par_id3166426	18			0	uk	Place the cursor in the field \<emph\>Variable Cell\</emph\>. In the sheet, click in the cell that contains the value to be changed, in this example it is the cell with the capital value \<item type=\"literal\"\>C\</item\>.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\goalseek.xhp	0	help	par_id3150369	19			0	uk	Enter the expected result of the formula in the \<emph\>Target Value\</emph\> text box. In this example, the value is 15,000. Click \<emph\>OK\</emph\>.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\goalseek.xhp	0	help	par_id3146978	20			0	uk	A dialog appears informing you that the Goal Seek was successful. Click \<emph\>Yes\</emph\> to enter the result in the cell with the variable value.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\goalseek.xhp	0	help	par_id3149409	23			0	uk	\<link href=\"text/scalc/01/06040000.xhp\" name=\"Goal Seek\"\>Goal Seek\</link\>				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\numbers_text.xhp	0	help	tit				0	uk	Converting Text to Numbers				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\numbers_text.xhp	0	help	bm_id3145068				0	uk	\<bookmark_value\>formats; text as numbers\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>time format conversion\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>date formats;conversion\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>converting;text, into numbers\</bookmark_value\>				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\numbers_text.xhp	0	help	hd_id0908200901265171				0	uk	\<variable id=\"numbers_text\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/numbers_text.xhp\" name=\"Converting Text to Numbers\"\>Converting Text to Numbers\</link\>\</variable\>				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\numbers_text.xhp	0	help	par_id0908200901265127				0	uk	Calc converts text inside cells to the respective numeric values if an unambiguous conversion is possible. If no conversion is possible, Calc returns a #VALUE! error.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\numbers_text.xhp	0	help	par_id0908200901265196				0	uk	Only integer numbers including exponent are converted, and ISO 8601 dates and times in their extended formats with separators. Anything else, like fractional numbers with decimal separators or dates other than ISO 8601, is not converted, as the text string would be locale dependent. Leading and trailing blanks are ignored.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\numbers_text.xhp	0	help	par_id0908200901265220				0	uk	The following ISO 8601 formats are converted:				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\numbers_text.xhp	0	help	par_id0908200901265288				0	uk	CCYY-MM-DD				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\numbers_text.xhp	0	help	par_id0908200901265267				0	uk	CCYY-MM-DDThh:mm				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\numbers_text.xhp	0	help	par_id0908200901265248				0	uk	CCYY-MM-DDThh:mm:ss				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\numbers_text.xhp	0	help	par_id0908200901265374				0	uk	CCYY-MM-DDThh:mm:ss,s				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\numbers_text.xhp	0	help	par_id0908200901265327				0	uk	CCYY-MM-DDThh:mm:ss.s				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\numbers_text.xhp	0	help	par_id0908200901265399				0	uk	hh:mm				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\numbers_text.xhp	0	help	par_id0908200901265347				0	uk	hh:mm:ss				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\numbers_text.xhp	0	help	par_id0908200901265349				0	uk	hh:mm:ss,s				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\numbers_text.xhp	0	help	par_id0908200901265342				0	uk	hh:mm:ss.s				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\numbers_text.xhp	0	help	par_id0908200901265491				0	uk	The century code CC may not be omitted. Instead of the T date and time separator, exactly one space character may be used.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\numbers_text.xhp	0	help	par_id0908200901265467				0	uk	If a date is given, it must be a valid Gregorian calendar date. In this case the optional time must be in the range 00:00 to 23:59:59.99999...				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\numbers_text.xhp	0	help	par_id0908200901265420				0	uk	If only a time string is given, it may have an hours value of more than 24, while minutes and seconds can have a maximum value of 59.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\numbers_text.xhp	0	help	par_id0908200901265448				0	uk	The conversion is done for single arguments only, as in =A1+A2, or ="1E2"+1. Cell range arguments are not affected, so SUM(A1:A2) differs from A1+A2 if at least one of the two cells contain a convertible string.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\numbers_text.xhp	0	help	par_id090820090126540				0	uk	Strings inside formulas are also converted, such as in ="1999-11-22"+42, which returns the date 42 days after November 22nd, 1999. Calculations involving localized dates as strings inside the formula return an error. For example, the localized date string "11/22/1999" or "22.11.1999" cannot be used for the automatic conversion.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\numbers_text.xhp	0	help	hd_id1005200903485368				0	uk	Example				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\numbers_text.xhp	0	help	par_id1005200903485359				0	uk	In A1 enter the text \<item type=\"literal\"\>'1e2\</item\> (which is converted to the number 100 internally).				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\numbers_text.xhp	0	help	par_id1005200903485341				0	uk	In A2 enter \<item type=\"literal\"\>=A1+1\</item\> (which correctly results in 101).				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\numbers_text.xhp	0	help	par_id0908200901265544				0	uk	\<link href=\"text/shared/01/05020300.xhp\" name=\"Format - Cells - Numbers\"\>Format - Cells - Numbers\</link\>				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\table_rotate.xhp	0	help	tit				0	uk	Rotating Tables (Transposing)				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\table_rotate.xhp	0	help	bm_id3154346				0	uk	\<bookmark_value\>tables; transposing\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>transposing tables\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>inverting tables\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>swapping tables\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>columns; swap with rows\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>rows; swapping with columns\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>tables; rotating\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>rotating; tables\</bookmark_value\>				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\table_rotate.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154346	1			0	uk	\<variable id=\"table_rotate\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/table_rotate.xhp\" name=\"Rotating Tables (Transposing)\"\>Rotating Tables (Transposing)\</link\>\</variable\>				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\table_rotate.xhp	0	help	par_id3154013	2			0	uk	In $[officename] Calc, there is a way to "rotate" a spreadsheet so that rows become columns and columns become rows.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\table_rotate.xhp	0	help	par_id3153142	3			0	uk	Select the cell range that you want to transpose.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\table_rotate.xhp	0	help	par_id3153191	4			0	uk	Choose \<emph\>Edit - Cut\</emph\>.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\table_rotate.xhp	0	help	par_id3148575	6			0	uk	Click the cell that is to be the top left cell in the result.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\table_rotate.xhp	0	help	par_id3156286	7			0	uk	Choose \<emph\>Edit - Paste Special\</emph\>.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\table_rotate.xhp	0	help	par_id3144764	8			0	uk	In the dialog, mark \<emph\>Paste all\</emph\> and \<emph\>Transpose\</emph\>.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\table_rotate.xhp	0	help	par_id3155600	9			0	uk	If you now click OK the columns and rows are transposed.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\table_rotate.xhp	0	help	par_id3146969	10			0	uk	\<link href=\"text/shared/01/02070000.xhp\" name=\"Paste Special\"\>Paste Special\</link\>				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_timevalues.xhp	0	help	tit				0	uk	Calculating Time Differences				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_timevalues.xhp	0	help	bm_id3150769				0	uk	\<bookmark_value\>calculating;time differences\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>time differences\</bookmark_value\>				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_timevalues.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150769	53			0	uk	\<variable id=\"calc_timevalues\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/calc_timevalues.xhp\" name=\"Calculating Time Differences\"\>Calculating Time Differences\</link\>\</variable\>				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_timevalues.xhp	0	help	par_id3149263	54			0	uk	If you want to calculate time differences, for example, the time between 23:30 and 01:10 in the same night, use the following formula:				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_timevalues.xhp	0	help	par_id3159153	55			0	uk	=(B2<A2)+B2-A2				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_timevalues.xhp	0	help	par_id3152598	56			0	uk	The later time is B2 and the earlier time is A2. The result of the example is 01:40 or 1 hour and 40 minutes.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_timevalues.xhp	0	help	par_id3145271	57			0	uk	In the formula, an entire 24-hour day has a value of 1 and one hour has a value of 1/24. The logical value in parentheses is 0 or 1, corresponding to 0 or 24 hours. The result returned by the formula is automatically issued in time format due to the sequence of the operands.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formula_copy.xhp	0	help	tit				0	uk	Copying Formulas				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formula_copy.xhp	0	help	bm_id3151113				0	uk	\<bookmark_value\>formulas; copying and pasting\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>copying; formulas\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>pasting;formulas\</bookmark_value\>				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formula_copy.xhp	0	help	hd_id3151113	54			0	uk	\<variable id=\"formula_copy\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/formula_copy.xhp\" name=\"Copying Formulas\"\>Copying Formulas\</link\>\</variable\>				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formula_copy.xhp	0	help	par_id3156424	11			0	uk	There are various ways to copy a formula. One suggested method is:				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formula_copy.xhp	0	help	par_id3150439	30			0	uk	Select the cell containing the formula.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formula_copy.xhp	0	help	par_id3154319	31			0	uk	Choose \<emph\>Edit - Copy\</emph\>, or press \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>Command\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Ctrl\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\>+C to copy it.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formula_copy.xhp	0	help	par_id3159155	32			0	uk	Select the cell into which you want the formula to be copied.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formula_copy.xhp	0	help	par_id3153728	33			0	uk	Choose \<emph\>Edit - Paste\</emph\>, or press \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>Command\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Ctrl\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\>+V. The formula will be positioned in the new cell.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formula_copy.xhp	0	help	par_id3149961	34			0	uk	If you want to copy a formula into multiple cells, there is a quick and easy way to copy into adjacent cell areas:				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formula_copy.xhp	0	help	par_id3149400	12			0	uk	Select the cell containing the formula.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formula_copy.xhp	0	help	par_id3154018	13			0	uk	Position the mouse on the bottom right of the highlighted border of the cell, and continue holding down the mouse button until the pointer changes to a cross-hair symbol.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formula_copy.xhp	0	help	par_id3150749	14			0	uk	With the mouse button pressed, drag it down or to the right over all the cells into which you want to copy the formula.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formula_copy.xhp	0	help	par_id3153714	15			0	uk	When you release the mouse button, the formula will be copied into the cells and automatically adjusted.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formula_copy.xhp	0	help	par_id3156385	53			0	uk	If you do not want values and texts to be automatically adjusted, then hold down the \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>Command\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Ctrl\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\> key when dragging. Formulas, however, are always adjusted accordingly.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\printranges.xhp	0	help	tit				0	uk	Using Print Ranges on a Spreadsheet				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\printranges.xhp	0	help	bm_id14648				0	uk	\<bookmark_value\>exporting;cells\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>printing; cells\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>ranges;print ranges\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>PDF export of print ranges\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>cell ranges; printing\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>cells; print ranges\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>print ranges\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>clearing, see also deleting/removing\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>defining;print ranges\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>extending print ranges\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>deleting;print ranges\</bookmark_value\>				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\printranges.xhp	0	help	par_idN108D7				0	uk	\<variable id=\"printranges\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/printranges.xhp\"\>Defining Print Ranges on a Sheet\</link\> \</variable\>				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\printranges.xhp	0	help	par_idN108F5				0	uk	You can define which range of cells on a spreadsheet to print.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\printranges.xhp	0	help	par_idN108FB				0	uk	The cells on the sheet that are not part of the defined print range are not printed or exported. Sheets without a defined print range are not printed and not exported to a PDF file, unless the document uses the Excel file format.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\printranges.xhp	0	help	par_idN1077A				0	uk	For files opened in Excel format, all sheets that do not contain a defined print range are printed. The same behavior occurs when you export the Excel formatted spreadsheet to a PDF file.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\printranges.xhp	0	help	par_idN108FE				0	uk	To Define a Print Range				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\printranges.xhp	0	help	par_idN10905				0	uk	Select the cells that you want to print.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\printranges.xhp	0	help	par_idN10909				0	uk	Choose \<emph\>Format - Print Ranges - Define\</emph\>.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\printranges.xhp	0	help	par_idN10910				0	uk	To Add Cells to a Print Range				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\printranges.xhp	0	help	par_idN10917				0	uk	Select the cells that you want to add to the existing print range.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\printranges.xhp	0	help	par_idN1091B				0	uk	Choose \<emph\>Format - Print Ranges - Add\</emph\>.				20220827 09:49:47
