helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\line_fix.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154684	1			0	uk	\<variable id=\"line_fix\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/line_fix.xhp\" name=\"Freezing Rows or Columns as Headers\"\>Freezing Rows or Columns as Headers\</link\>\</variable\>				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\line_fix.xhp	0	help	par_id3148576	2			0	uk	If you have long rows or columns of data that extend beyond the viewable area of the sheet, you can freeze some rows or columns, which allows you to see the frozen columns or rows as you scroll through the rest of the data.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\line_fix.xhp	0	help	par_id3156441	3			0	uk	Select the row below, or the column to the right of the row or column that you want to be in the frozen region. All rows above, or all columns to the left of the selection are frozen.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\line_fix.xhp	0	help	par_id3153158	13			0	uk	To freeze both horizontally and vertically, select the \<emph\>cell\</emph\> that is below the row and to the right of the column that you want to freeze.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\line_fix.xhp	0	help	par_id3156286	4			0	uk	Choose \<emph\>Window - Freeze\</emph\>.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\line_fix.xhp	0	help	par_id3151073	5			0	uk	To deactivate, choose \<emph\>Window - Freeze \</emph\>again.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\line_fix.xhp	0	help	par_id3155335	7			0	uk	If the area defined is to be scrollable, apply the \<emph\>Window - Split\</emph\> command.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\line_fix.xhp	0	help	par_id3147345	8			0	uk	If you want to print a certain row on all pages of a document, choose \<emph\>Format - Print ranges - Edit\</emph\>.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\line_fix.xhp	0	help	par_id3147004	9			0	uk	\<link href=\"text/scalc/01/07090000.xhp\" name=\"Window - Freeze\"\>Window - Freeze\</link\>				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\line_fix.xhp	0	help	par_id3150088	10			0	uk	\<link href=\"text/scalc/01/07080000.xhp\" name=\"Window - Split\"\>Window - Split\</link\>				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\line_fix.xhp	0	help	par_id3150304	11			0	uk	\<link href=\"text/scalc/01/05080300.xhp\" name=\"Format - Print ranges - Edit\"\>Format - Print ranges - Edit\</link\>				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	tit				0	uk	Applying Conditional Formatting				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	bm_id3149263				0	uk	\<bookmark_value\>conditional formatting; cells\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>cells; conditional formatting\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>formatting; conditional formatting\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>styles;conditional styles\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>cell formats; conditional\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>random numbers;examples\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>cell styles; copying\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>copying; cell styles\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>tables; copying cell styles\</bookmark_value\>				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149263	24			0	uk	\<variable id=\"cellstyle_conditional\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/cellstyle_conditional.xhp\" name=\"Applying Conditional Formatting\"\>Applying Conditional Formatting\</link\>\</variable\>				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	par_id3159156	25			0	uk	Using the menu command \<emph\>Format - Conditional formatting\</emph\>, the dialog allows you to define up to three conditions per cell, which must be met in order for the selected cells to have a particular format.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	par_id8039796				0	uk	To apply conditional formatting, AutoCalculate must be enabled. Choose \<emph\>Tools - Cell Contents - AutoCalculate\</emph\> (you see a check mark next to the command when AutoCalculate is enabled).				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	par_id3154944	26			0	uk	With conditional formatting, you can, for example, highlight the totals that exceed the average value of all totals. If the totals change, the formatting changes correspondingly, without having to apply other styles manually.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	hd_id4480727				0	uk	To Define the Conditions				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	par_id3154490	27			0	uk	Select the cells to which you want to apply a conditional style.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	par_id3155603	28			0	uk	Choose \<emph\>Format - Conditional Formatting\</emph\>.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	par_id3146969	29			0	uk	Enter the condition(s) into the dialog box. The dialog is described in detail in \<link href=\"text/scalc/01/05120000.xhp\" name=\"$[officename] Help\"\>$[officename] Help\</link\>, and an example is provided below:				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155766	38			0	uk	Example of Conditional Formatting: Highlighting Totals Above/Under the Average Value				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	hd_id4341868				0	uk	Step1: Generate Number Values				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	par_id3150043	39			0	uk	You want to give certain values in your tables particular emphasis. For example, in a table of turnovers, you can show all the values above the average in green and all those below the average in red. This is possible with conditional formatting.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	par_id3155337	40			0	uk	First of all, write a table in which a few different values occur. For your test you can create tables with any random numbers:				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	par_id3149565	41			0	uk	In one of the cells enter the formula =RAND(), and you will obtain a random number between 0 and 1. If you want integers of between 0 and 50, enter the formula =INT(RAND()*50).				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	par_id3149258	42			0	uk	Copy the formula to create a row of random numbers. Click the bottom right corner of the selected cell, and drag to the right until the desired cell range is selected.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	par_id3159236	43			0	uk	In the same way as described above, drag down the corner of the rightmost cell in order to create more rows of random numbers.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149211	44			0	uk	Step 2: Define Cell Styles				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	par_id3154659	45			0	uk	The next step is to apply a cell style to all values that represent above-average turnover, and one to those that are below the average. Ensure that the Styles and Formatting window is visible before proceeding.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	par_id3150883	46			0	uk	Click in a blank cell and select the command \<emph\>Format Cells\</emph\> in the context menu.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	par_id3155529	47			0	uk	In the \<emph\>Format Cells\</emph\> dialog on the \<emph\>Background\</emph\> tab, select a background color. Click \<emph\>OK\</emph\>.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	par_id3154484	48			0	uk	In the Styles and Formatting window, click the \<emph\>New Style from Selection\</emph\> icon. Enter the name of the new style. For this example, name the style "Above".				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	par_id3152889	49			0	uk	To define a second style, click again in a blank cell and proceed as described above. Assign a different background color for the cell and assign a name (for this example, "Below").				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148704	60			0	uk	Step 3: Calculate Average				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	par_id3148837	51			0	uk	In our particular example, we are calculating the average of the random values. The result is placed in a cell:				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	par_id3144768	52			0	uk	Set the cursor in a blank cell, for example, J14, and choose \<emph\>Insert - Function\</emph\>.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	par_id3156016	53			0	uk	Select the AVERAGE function. Use the mouse to select all your random numbers. If you cannot see the entire range, because the Function Wizard is obscuring it, you can temporarily shrink the dialog using the \<link href=\"text/shared/00/00000001.xhp#eingabesymbol\" name=\"Shrink or Maximize\"\>\<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Shrink / Maximize\</item\>\</link\> icon.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	par_id3153246	54			0	uk	Close the Function Wizard with \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>OK\</item\>.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149898	50			0	uk	Step 4: Apply Cell Styles				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	par_id3149126	55			0	uk	Now you can apply the conditional formatting to the sheet:				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	par_id3150049	56			0	uk	Select all cells with the random numbers.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	par_id3153801	57			0	uk	Choose the \<emph\>Format - Conditional Formatting\</emph\> command to open the corresponding dialog.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	par_id3153013	58			0	uk	Define the condition as follows: If cell value is less than J14, format with cell style "Below", and if cell value is greater than or equal to J14, format with cell style "Above".				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155761	61			0	uk	Step 5: Copy Cell Style				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	par_id3145320	62			0	uk	To apply the conditional formatting to other cells later:				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	par_id3153074	63			0	uk	Click one of the cells that has been assigned conditional formatting.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	par_id3149051	64			0	uk	Copy the cell to the clipboard.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	par_id3150436	65			0	uk	Select the cells that are to receive this same formatting.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	par_id3147298	66			0	uk	Choose \<emph\>Edit - Paste Special\</emph\>. The \<emph\>Paste Special\</emph\> dialog appears.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	par_id3166465	67			0	uk	In the \<emph\>Selection\</emph\> area, check only the \<emph\>Formats\</emph\> box. All other boxes must be unchecked. Click \<emph\>OK\</emph\>.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	par_id3159123	68			0	uk	\<link href=\"text/scalc/01/05120000.xhp\" name=\"Format - Conditional formatting\"\>Format - Conditional formatting\</link\>				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot_filtertable.xhp	0	help	tit				0	uk	Filtering Pivot Tables				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot_filtertable.xhp	0	help	bm_id3150792				0	uk	\<bookmark_value\>pivot table function; filtering tables\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>filtering;pivot tables\</bookmark_value\>				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot_filtertable.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150792				0	uk	\<variable id=\"datapilot_filtertable\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/datapilot_filtertable.xhp\" name=\"Filtering Pivot Tables\"\>Filtering Pivot Tables\</link\>\</variable\>				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot_filtertable.xhp	0	help	par_id3153192				0	uk	You can use filters to remove unwanted data from a pivot table.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot_filtertable.xhp	0	help	par_id3150441				0	uk	Click the \<emph\>Filter\</emph\> button in the sheet to call up the dialog for the filter conditions. Alternatively, call up the context menu of the pivot table and select the \<emph\>Filter\</emph\> command. The \<link href=\"text/scalc/01/12090103.xhp\" name=\"Filter\"\>\<emph\>Filter\</emph\>\</link\> dialog appears. Here you can filter the pivot table.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot_filtertable.xhp	0	help	par_id315044199				0	uk	You can also click the arrow on a button in the pivot table to show a pop-up window. In this pop-up window, you can edit the visibility settings of the associated field.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot_filtertable.xhp	0	help	par_id0720201001344485				0	uk	The pop-up window displays a list of field members associated with that field. A check box is placed to the left of each field member name. When a field has an alternative display name that differs from its original name, that name is displayed in the list.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot_filtertable.xhp	0	help	par_id0720201001344449				0	uk	Enable or disable a checkbox to show or hide the associated field member in the pivot table.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot_filtertable.xhp	0	help	par_id0720201001344493				0	uk	Enable or disable the \<emph\>All\</emph\> checkbox to show all or none of the field members.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot_filtertable.xhp	0	help	par_id0720201001344431				0	uk	Select a field member in the pop-up window and click the \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Show only the current item\</item\> button to show only the selected field member. All other field members are hidden in the pivot table.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot_filtertable.xhp	0	help	par_id0720201001344484				0	uk	Select a field member in the pop-up window and click the \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Hide only the current item\</item\> button to hide only the selected field member. All other field members are shown in the pivot table.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot_filtertable.xhp	0	help	par_id0720201001344578				0	uk	Commands enable you to sort the field members in ascending order, descending order, or using a custom sort list.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot_filtertable.xhp	0	help	par_id0720201001344584				0	uk	To edit the custom sort lists, open \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>%PRODUCTNAME - Preferences\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Tools - Options\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\> - %PRODUCTNAME Calc - Sort Lists.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot_filtertable.xhp	0	help	par_id0720201001344811				0	uk	The arrow to open the pop-up window is normally black. When the field contains one or more hidden field members, the arrow is blue and displays a tiny square at its lower-right corner.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot_filtertable.xhp	0	help	par_id0720201001344884				0	uk	You can also open the pop-up window by positioning the cell cursor at the button and pressing \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>Command\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Ctrl\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\>+D.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_by_formula.xhp	0	help	tit				0	uk	Assigning Formats by Formula				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_by_formula.xhp	0	help	bm_id3145673				0	uk	\<bookmark_value\>formats; assigning by formulas\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>cell formats; assigning by formulas\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>STYLE function example\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>cell styles;assigning by formulas\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>formulas;assigning cell formats\</bookmark_value\>				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_by_formula.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145673	13			0	uk	\<variable id=\"cellstyle_by_formula\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/cellstyle_by_formula.xhp\" name=\"Assigning Formats by Formula\"\>Assigning Formats by Formula\</link\> \</variable\>				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_by_formula.xhp	0	help	par_id3150275	14			0	uk	The STYLE() function can be added to an existing formula in a cell. For example, together with the CURRENT function, you can color a cell depending on its value. The formula =...+STYLE(IF(CURRENT()>3; "Red"; "Green")) applies the cell style "Red" to cells if the value is greater than 3, otherwise the cell style "Green" is applied.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_by_formula.xhp	0	help	par_id3151385	15			0	uk	If you would like to apply a formula to all cells in a selected area, you can use the \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Find & Replace\</item\> dialog.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_by_formula.xhp	0	help	par_id3149456	16			0	uk	Select all the desired cells.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_by_formula.xhp	0	help	par_id3148797	17			0	uk	Select the menu command \<emph\>Edit - Find & Replace\</emph\>.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_by_formula.xhp	0	help	par_id3150767	18			0	uk	For the \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Search for\</item\> term, enter: .\<item type=\"literal\"\>*\</item\>            				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_by_formula.xhp	0	help	par_id3153770	19			0	uk	".*" is a regular expression that designates the contents of the current cell.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_by_formula.xhp	0	help	par_id3153143	20			0	uk	Enter the following formula in the \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Replace with\</item\> field: \<item type=\"literal\"\>=&+STYLE(IF(CURRENT()>3;"Red";"Green"))\</item\>            				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_by_formula.xhp	0	help	par_id3146975	21			0	uk	The "&" symbol designates the current contents of the \<emph\>Search for\</emph\> field. The line must begin with an equal sign, since it is a formula. It is assumed that the cell styles "Red" and "Green" already exist.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_by_formula.xhp	0	help	par_id3149262	22			0	uk	Mark the fields \<link href=\"text/shared/01/02100000.xhp\" name=\"Regular expressions\"\>\<emph\>Regular expressions\</emph\>\</link\> and \<emph\>Current selection only\</emph\>. Click \<emph\>Find All\</emph\>.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_by_formula.xhp	0	help	par_id3144767	24			0	uk	All cells with contents that were included in the selection are now highlighted.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_by_formula.xhp	0	help	par_id3147127	23			0	uk	Click \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Replace all\</item\>.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	tit				0	uk	Automatically Calculating Series				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	bm_id3150769				0	uk	\<bookmark_value\>series; calculating\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>calculating; series\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>linear series\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>growth series\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>date series\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>powers of 2 calculations\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>cells; filling automatically\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>automatic cell filling\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>AutoFill function\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>filling;cells, automatically\</bookmark_value\>				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150769	6			0	uk	\<variable id=\"calc_series\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/calc_series.xhp\" name=\"Automatically Calculating Series\"\>Automatically Filling in Data Based on Adjacent Cells\</link\>\</variable\>				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_idN106A8				0	uk	You can automatically fill cells with data with the AutoFill command or the Series command.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_idN106D3				0	uk	Using AutoFill				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_idN106D7				0	uk	AutoFill automatically generates a data series based on a defined pattern.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_id3154319	7			0	uk	On a sheet, click in a cell, and type a number.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_idN106CB				0	uk	Click in another cell and then click back in the cell where you typed the number.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_id3145272	16			0	uk	Drag the fill handle in the bottom right corner of the cell across the cells that you want to fill, and release the mouse button.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_id3145801	17			0	uk	The cells are filled with ascending numbers.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_idN106EE				0	uk	To quickly create a list of consecutive days, enter \<item type=\"literal\"\>Monday\</item\> in a cell, and drag the fill handle.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_id9720145				0	uk	Hold down \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>Command \</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Ctrl \</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\>if you do not want to fill the cells with different values.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_id3154490	18			0	uk	If you select two or more adjacent cells that contain different numbers, and drag, the remaining cells are filled with the arithmetic pattern that is recognized in the numbers. The AutoFill function also recognizes customized lists that are defined under \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>\<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>%PRODUCTNAME - Preferences\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Tools - Options\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\> - %PRODUCTNAME Calc - Sort Lists\</item\>.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_idN10737				0	uk	You can double-click the fill handle to automatically fill all empty columns of the current data block. For example, first enter Jan into A1 and drag the fill handle down to A12 to get the twelve months in the first column. Now enter some values into B1 and C1. Select those two cells, and double-click the fill handle. This fills automatically the data block B1:C12.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_idN10713				0	uk	Using a Defined Series				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_id3150749	9			0	uk	Select the cell range in the sheet that you want to fill.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_id3154754	19			0	uk	Choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Edit - Fill - Series\</item\>.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_idN10716				0	uk	Select the parameters for the series. 				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_idN10731				0	uk	If you select a \<emph\>linear\</emph\> series, the increment that you enter is \<emph\>added\</emph\> to each consecutive number in the series to create the next value.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_idN1073C				0	uk	If you select a \<emph\>growth\</emph\> series, the increment that you enter is \<emph\>multiplied\</emph\> by each consecutive number to create the next value.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_idN10747				0	uk	If you select a \<emph\>date\</emph\> series, the increment that you enter is added to the time unit that you specify.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_id3159173	20			0	uk	\<link href=\"text/shared/optionen/01060400.xhp\" name=\"Sort lists\"\>Sort lists\</link\>				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_define.xhp	0	help	tit				0	uk	Defining Database Ranges				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_define.xhp	0	help	bm_id3154758				0	uk	\<bookmark_value\>tables; database ranges\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>database ranges; defining\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>ranges; defining database ranges\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>defining;database ranges\</bookmark_value\>				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_define.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154758	31			0	uk	\<variable id=\"database_define\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/database_define.xhp\" name=\"Defining Database Ranges\"\>Defining a Database Range\</link\>\</variable\>				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_define.xhp	0	help	par_id3153768	81			0	uk	You can define a range of cells in a spreadsheet to use as a database. Each row in this database range corresponds to a database record and each cell in a row corresponds to a database field. You can sort, group, search, and perform calculations on the range as you would in a database.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_define.xhp	0	help	par_id3145801	82			0	uk	You can only edit and access a database range in the spreadsheet that contains the range. You cannot access the database range in the %PRODUCTNAME Data Sources view. 				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_define.xhp	0	help	par_idN10648				0	uk	To define a database range				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_define.xhp	0	help	par_id3155064	41			0	uk	Select the range of cells that you want to define as a database range.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_define.xhp	0	help	par_idN10654				0	uk	Choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Data - Define Range\</item\>.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_define.xhp	0	help	par_id3153715	72			0	uk	In the \<emph\>Name\</emph\> box, enter a name for the database range.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_define.xhp	0	help	par_idN1066A				0	uk	Натисніть \<emph\>Гаразд\</emph\>.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_define.xhp	0	help	par_id3154253	42			0	uk	Specify the options for the database range.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_define.xhp	0	help	par_idN10675				0	uk	Натисніть \<emph\>Гаразд\</emph\>.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot_edittable.xhp	0	help	tit				0	uk	Editing Pivot Tables				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot_edittable.xhp	0	help	bm_id3148663				0	uk	\<bookmark_value\>pivot table function; editing tables\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>editing;pivot tables\</bookmark_value\>				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot_edittable.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148663	25			0	uk	\<variable id=\"datapilot_edittable\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/datapilot_edittable.xhp\" name=\"Editing Pivot Tables\"\>Editing Pivot Tables\</link\>\</variable\>				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot_edittable.xhp	0	help	par_id3150868	26			0	uk	Click one of the buttons in the pivot table and hold the mouse button down. A special symbol will appear next to the mouse pointer.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot_edittable.xhp	0	help	par_id3145786	27			0	uk	By dragging the button to a different position in the same row you can alter the order of the columns. If you drag a button to the left edge of the table into the row headings area, you can change a column into a row.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot_edittable.xhp	0	help	par_id1648915				0	uk	In the Pivot Table dialog, you can drag a button to the \<emph\>Page Fields\</emph\> area to create a button and a listbox on top of the pivot table. The listbox can be used to filter the pivot table by the contents of the selected item. You can use drag-and-drop within the pivot table to use another page field as a filter.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot_edittable.xhp	0	help	par_id3147434	29			0	uk	To remove a button from the table, just drag it out of the pivot table. Release the mouse button when the mouse pointer positioned within the sheet has become a 'not allowed' icon. The button is deleted.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot_edittable.xhp	0	help	par_id3156442	40			0	uk	To edit the pivot table, click a cell inside the pivot table and open the context menu. In the context menu you find the command \<emph\>Edit Layout\</emph\>, which displays the \<emph\>Pivot Table\</emph\> dialog for the current pivot table.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot_edittable.xhp	0	help	par_id2666096				0	uk	In the pivot table, you can use drag-and-drop or cut/paste commands to rearrange the order of data fields.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot_edittable.xhp	0	help	par_id266609688				0	uk	You can assign custom display names to fields, field members, subtotals (with some restrictions), and grand totals inside pivot tables. A custom display name is assigned to an item by overwriting the original name with another name.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_title_row.xhp	0	help	tit				0	uk	Printing Rows or Columns on Every Page				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_title_row.xhp	0	help	bm_id3151112				0	uk	\<bookmark_value\>printing; sheets on multiple pages\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>sheets; printing on multiple pages\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>rows; repeating when printing\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>columns; repeating when printing\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>repeating;columns/rows on printed pages\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>title rows; printing on all sheets\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>headers; printing on sheets\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>footers; printing on sheets\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>printing; rows/columns as table headings\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>headings;repeating rows/columns as\</bookmark_value\>				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_title_row.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153727	21			0	uk	\<variable id=\"print_title_row\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/print_title_row.xhp\" name=\"Printing Rows or Columns on Every Page\"\>Printing Rows or Columns on Every Page\</link\>\</variable\>				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_title_row.xhp	0	help	par_id3154014	2			0	uk	If you have a sheet that is so large that it will be printed multiple pages, you can set up rows or columns to repeat on each printed page.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_title_row.xhp	0	help	par_id3146975	7			0	uk	As an example, If you want to print the top two rows of the sheet as well as the first column (A)on all pages, do the following:				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_title_row.xhp	0	help	par_id3163710	8			0	uk	Choose \<emph\>Format - Print Ranges - Edit\</emph\>. The \<emph\>Edit Print Ranges\</emph\> dialog appears.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_title_row.xhp	0	help	par_id3149958	9			0	uk	Click the icon at the far right of the \<emph\>Rows to repeat\</emph\> area.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_title_row.xhp	0	help	par_id3145800	10			0	uk	The dialog shrinks so that you can see more of the sheet.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_title_row.xhp	0	help	par_id3155602	11			0	uk	Select the first two rows and, for this example, click cell A1 and drag to A2.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_title_row.xhp	0	help	par_id3154018	12			0	uk	In the shrunk dialog you will see $1:$2. Rows 1 and 2 are now rows to repeat.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_title_row.xhp	0	help	par_id3153707	13			0	uk	Click the icon at the far right of the \<emph\>Rows to repeat\</emph\> area. The dialog is restored again.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_title_row.xhp	0	help	par_id3155443	14			0	uk	If you also want column A as a column to repeat, click the icon at the far right of the \<emph\>Columns to repeat\</emph\> area.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_title_row.xhp	0	help	par_id3154256	15			0	uk	Click column A (not in the column header).				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_title_row.xhp	0	help	par_id3154704	16			0	uk	Click the icon again at the far right of the \<emph\>Columns to repeat\</emph\> area.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_title_row.xhp	0	help	par_id3150088	17			0	uk	Rows to repeat are rows from the sheet. You can define headers and footers to be printed on each print page independently of this in \<emph\>Format - Page\</emph\>.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_title_row.xhp	0	help	par_id3155380	18			0	uk	\<link href=\"text/scalc/01/03100000.xhp\" name=\"View - Page Break Preview\"\>View - Page Break Preview\</link\>				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_title_row.xhp	0	help	par_id3154371	19			0	uk	\<link href=\"text/scalc/01/05080300.xhp\" name=\"Format - Print ranges - Edit\"\>Format - Print ranges - Edit\</link\>				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_title_row.xhp	0	help	par_id3146113	20			0	uk	\<link href=\"text/scalc/01/05070000.xhp\" name=\"Format - Page - (Header / Footer)\"\>Format - Page - (Header / Footer)\</link\>				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\sorted_list.xhp	0	help	tit				0	uk	Applying Sort Lists				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\sorted_list.xhp	0	help	bm_id3150870				0	uk	\<bookmark_value\>filling;customized lists\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>sort lists;applying\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>defining;sort lists\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>geometric lists\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>arithmetic lists\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>series;sort lists\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>lists; user-defined\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>customized lists\</bookmark_value\>				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\sorted_list.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150870	3			0	uk	\<variable id=\"sorted_list\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/sorted_list.xhp\" name=\"Applying Sort Lists\"\>Applying Sort Lists\</link\> \</variable\>				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\sorted_list.xhp	0	help	par_id3159154	7			0	uk	Sort lists allow you to type one piece of information in a cell, then drag it to fill in a consecutive list of items.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\sorted_list.xhp	0	help	par_id3148645	4			0	uk	For example, enter the text "Jan" or "January" in an empty cell. Select the cell and click the mouse on the lower right corner of the cell border. Then drag the selected cell a few cells to the right or downwards. When you release the mouse button, the highlighted cells will be filled with the names of the months.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\sorted_list.xhp	0	help	par_id2367931				0	uk	Hold down \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>Command \</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Ctrl \</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\>if you do not want to fill the cells with different values.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\sorted_list.xhp	0	help	par_id3152577	5			0	uk	The predefined series can be found under \<emph\>\<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>%PRODUCTNAME - Preferences\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Tools - Options\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\> - %PRODUCTNAME Calc - Sort Lists\</emph\>. You can also create your own lists of text strings tailored to your needs, such as a list of your company's branch offices. When you use the information in these lists later (for example, as headings), just enter the first name in the list and expand the entry by dragging it with your mouse.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\sorted_list.xhp	0	help	par_id3147434	6			0	uk	\<link href=\"text/shared/optionen/01060400.xhp\" name=\"Sort lists\"\>Sort lists\</link\>				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\text_numbers.xhp	0	help	tit				0	uk	Formatting Numbers as Text				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\text_numbers.xhp	0	help	bm_id3145068				0	uk	\<bookmark_value\>numbers;entering as text\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>text formats; for numbers\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>formats; numbers as text\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>cell formats; text/numbers\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>formatting;numbers as text\</bookmark_value\>				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\text_numbers.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145068	46			0	uk	\<variable id=\"text_numbers\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/text_numbers.xhp\" name=\"Formatting Numbers as Text\"\>Formatting Numbers as Text\</link\>\</variable\>				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\text_numbers.xhp	0	help	par_id3156280	43			0	uk	You can format numbers as text in $[officename] Calc. Open the context menu of a cell or range of cells and choose \<emph\>Format Cells - Numbers\</emph\>, then select "Text" from the \<emph\>Category\</emph\> list. Any numbers subsequently entered into the formatted range are interpreted as text. The display of these "numbers" is left-justified, just as with other text.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\text_numbers.xhp	0	help	par_id3149377	44			0	uk	If you have already entered normal numbers in cells and have afterwards changed the format of the cells to "Text", the numbers will remain normal numbers. They will not be converted. Only numbers entered afterwards, or numbers which are then edited, will become text numbers.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\text_numbers.xhp	0	help	par_id3144765	45			0	uk	If you decide to enter a number directly as text, enter an apostrophe (') first. For example, for years in column headings, you can enter '1999, '2000 and '2001. The apostrophe is not visible in the cell, it only indicates that the entry is to be recognized as a text. This is useful if, for example, you enter a telephone number or postal code that begins with a zero (0), because a zero (0) at the start of a sequence of digits is removed in normal number formats.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\text_numbers.xhp	0	help	par_id3156284	47			0	uk	\<link href=\"text/shared/01/05020300.xhp\" name=\"Format - Cells - Numbers\"\>Format - Cells - Numbers\</link\>				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calculate.xhp	0	help	tit				0	uk	Calculating in Spreadsheets				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calculate.xhp	0	help	bm_id3150791				0	uk	\<bookmark_value\>spreadsheets; calculating\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>calculating; spreadsheets\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>formulas; calculating\</bookmark_value\>				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calculate.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150791				0	uk	\<variable id=\"calculate\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/calculate.xhp\" name=\"Calculating in Spreadsheets\"\>Calculating in Spreadsheets\</link\>\</variable\>				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calculate.xhp	0	help	par_id3146120				0	uk	The following is an example of a calculation in $[officename] Calc.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calculate.xhp	0	help	par_id3153951				0	uk	Click in a cell, and type a number				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calculate.xhp	0	help	par_idN10656				0	uk	Press Enter.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calculate.xhp	0	help	par_idN1065D				0	uk	The cursor moves down to the next cell.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calculate.xhp	0	help	par_id3155064				0	uk	Enter another number.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calculate.xhp	0	help	par_idN1066F				0	uk	Press the Tab key.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calculate.xhp	0	help	par_idN10676				0	uk	The cursor moves to the right into the next cell.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calculate.xhp	0	help	par_id3154253				0	uk	Type in a formula, for example, \<item type=\"literal\"\>=A3 * A4 / 100.\</item\>				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calculate.xhp	0	help	par_idN1068B				0	uk	 Press Enter.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calculate.xhp	0	help	par_id3147343				0	uk	The result of the formula appears in the cell. If you want, you can edit the formula in the input line of the Formula bar.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calculate.xhp	0	help	par_id3155378				0	uk	When you edit a formula, the new result is calculated automatically.				20220827 09:49:47
