helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149260	6			0	uk	Defining A Function Using %PRODUCTNAME Basic				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	par_id3148456	7			0	uk	Choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Tools - Macros - Organize Macros - %PRODUCTNAME Basic\</item\>.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	par_id3154510	8			0	uk	Click the \<emph\>Edit\</emph\> button. You will now see the Basic IDE.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	par_id3150327	9			0	uk	Enter the function code. In this example, we define a \<item type=\"literal\"\>VOL(a; b; c)\</item\> function that calculates the volume of a rectangular solid with side lengths \<item type=\"literal\"\>a\</item\>, \<item type=\"literal\"\>b\</item\> and \<item type=\"literal\"\>c\</item\>:				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	par_id3155443	10			0	uk	Close the Basic-IDE window.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	par_id3150043	11			0	uk	Your function is automatically saved in the default module and is now available. If you apply the function in a Calc document that is to be used on another computer, you can copy the function to the Calc document as described in the next section.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147340	18			0	uk	Copying a Function To a Document				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	par_id3145232	19			0	uk	In stage 2 of "Defining A Function Using %PRODUCTNAME Basic", in the \<emph\>Macro\</emph\> dialog you clicked on \<emph\>Edit \</emph\>. As the default, in the \<emph\>Macro from\</emph\> field the \<emph\>My Macros - Standard - Module1\</emph\> module is selected. The \<emph\>Standard\</emph\> library resides locally in your user directory.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	par_id3154022	20			0	uk	If you want to copy the user-defined function to a Calc document:				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	par_id3150304	21			0	uk	Choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Tools - Macros - Organize Macros - %PRODUCTNAME Basic\</item\> .				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	par_id3150086	22			0	uk	In the \<emph\>Macro from\</emph\> field select \<emph\>My Macros - Standard - Module1\</emph\> and click \<emph\>Edit\</emph\>.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	par_id3166430	23			0	uk	In the Basic-IDE, select the source of your user-defined function and copy it to the clipboard.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	par_idN1081D				0	uk	Close the Basic-IDE.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	par_id3150517	24			0	uk	Choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Tools - Macros - Organize Macros - %PRODUCTNAME Basic\</item\> .				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	par_id3145384	25			0	uk	In the \<emph\>Macro from\</emph\> field select \<emph\>(Name of the Calc document) - Standard - Module1\</emph\>. Click \<emph\>Edit\</emph\>.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	par_id3148699	26			0	uk	Paste the clipboard contents in the Basic-IDE of the document.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153305	12			0	uk	Applying a User-defined Function in $[officename] Calc				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	par_id3148869	13			0	uk	Once you have defined the function \<item type=\"literal\"\>VOL(a; b; c)\</item\> in the Basic-IDE, you can apply it the same way as the built-in functions of $[officename] Calc.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	par_id3148606	14			0	uk	Open a Calc document and enter numbers for the function parameters \<item type=\"literal\"\>a\</item\>, \<item type=\"literal\"\>b\</item\>, and \<item type=\"literal\"\>c\</item\> in cells A1, B1, and C1.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	par_id3156019	15			0	uk	Set the cursor in another cell and enter the following:				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	par_id3155264	16			0	uk	=VOL(A1;B1;C1)				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	par_id3146776	17			0	uk	The function is evaluated and you will see the result in the selected cell.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\multi_tables.xhp	0	help	tit				0	uk	Navigating Through Sheets Tabs				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\multi_tables.xhp	0	help	bm_id3150769				0	uk	\<bookmark_value\>sheets; showing multiple\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>sheet tabs;using\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>views;multiple sheets\</bookmark_value\>				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\multi_tables.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150769	4			0	uk	\<variable id=\"multi_tables\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/multi_tables.xhp\" name=\"Navigating Through Sheet Tabs\"\>Navigating Through Sheet Tabs\</link\> \</variable\>				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\multi_tables.xhp	0	help	par_id3153771	3			0	uk	By default $[officename] displays three sheets "Sheet1" to "Sheet3", in each new spreadsheet. You can switch between sheets in a spreadsheet using the sheet tabs at the bottom of the screen.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\multi_tables.xhp	0	help	par_idN106AF				0	uk	 \<image id=\"img_id4829822\" src=\"res/helpimg/sheettabs.png\" width=\"3.3335inch\" height=\"0.7638inch\" localize=\"true\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id4829822\"\>Sheet Tabs\</alt\>\</image\>				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\multi_tables.xhp	0	help	par_id3153144				0	uk	 \<image id=\"img_id3156441\" src=\"res/helpimg/calcnav.png\" width=\"0.6563inch\" height=\"0.1457inch\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id3156441\"\>Icon\</alt\>\</image\>				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\multi_tables.xhp	0	help	par_id3147396	5			0	uk	Use the navigation buttons to display all the sheets belonging to your document. Clicking the button on the far left or the far right displays, respectively, the first or last sheet tab. The middle buttons allow the user to scroll forward and backward through all sheet tabs. To display the sheet itself click on the sheet tab.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\multi_tables.xhp	0	help	par_id3149379	6			0	uk	If there is insufficient space to display all the sheet tabs, you can increase it by pointing to the separator between the scrollbar and the sheet tabs, pressing the mouse button and, keeping the mouse button pressed, dragging to the right. In doing so you will be sharing the available space between the sheet tabs and horizontal scrollbar.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot_tipps.xhp	0	help	tit				0	uk	Selecting Pivot Table Output Ranges				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot_tipps.xhp	0	help	bm_id3148663				0	uk	\<bookmark_value\>pivot table function; preventing data overwriting\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>output ranges of pivot tables\</bookmark_value\>				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot_tipps.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148663	19			0	uk	\<variable id=\"datapilot_tipps\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/datapilot_tipps.xhp\" name=\"Selecting Pivot Table Output Ranges\"\>Selecting Pivot Table Output Ranges\</link\>\</variable\>				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot_tipps.xhp	0	help	par_id3154123	20			0	uk	Click the button \<emph\>More\</emph\> in the \<emph\>Pivot Table\</emph\> dialog. The dialog will be extended.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot_tipps.xhp	0	help	par_id3153771	21			0	uk	You can select a named range in which the pivot table is to be created, from the \<emph\>Results to\</emph\> box. If the results range does not have a name, enter the coordinates of the upper left cell of the range into the field to the right of the \<emph\>Results to\</emph\> box. You can also click on the appropriate cell to have the coordinates entered accordingly.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot_tipps.xhp	0	help	par_id3146974	23			0	uk	If you mark the \<emph\>Ignore empty rows\</emph\> check box, they will not be taken into account when the pivot table is created.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot_tipps.xhp	0	help	par_id3145273	24			0	uk	If the \<emph\>Identify categories\</emph\> check box is marked, the categories will be identified by their headings and assigned accordingly when the pivot table is created.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\row_height.xhp	0	help	tit				0	uk	Changing Row Height or Column Width				20220827 09:49:47
