helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3149762	20			0	uk	0.##				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149276	230			0	uk	Thousands Separator				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3154380	21			0	uk	Depending on your language setting, you can use a comma or a period as a thousands separator. You can also use the separator to reduce the size of the number that is displayed by a multiple of 1000.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3154905	22			0	uk	Number Format				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3150822	23			0	uk	Format Code				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3147264	24			0	uk	15000 as 15,000				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3151223	25			0	uk	#,###				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3154935	26			0	uk	16000 as 16				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3153961	27			0	uk	#,				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154836	79			0	uk	Including Text in Number Format Codes				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150398	231			0	uk	Text and Numbers				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3154224	80			0	uk	To include text in a number format that is applied to a cell containing numbers, place a double quotation mark (") in front of and behind the text, or a backslash (\\) before a single character. For example, enter \<emph\>#.# "meters"\</emph\> to display "3.5 meters" or \<emph\>#.# \\m\</emph\> to display "3.5 m".				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148979	232			0	uk	Text and Text				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3153338	82			0	uk	To include text in a number format that is applied to a cell that might contain text, enclose the text by double quotation marks (" "), and then add an at sign (@). For example, enter \<emph\>"Total for "@\</emph\> to display "Total for December".				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154330	233			0	uk	Spaces				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3156294	81			0	uk	To use a character to define the width of a space in a number format, type an underscore ( _ ) followed by the character. The width of the space varies according to the width of the character that you choose. For example, \<emph\>_M\</emph\> creates a wider space than \<emph\>_i\</emph\>.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155994	234			0	uk	Color				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3156423	28			0	uk	To set the color of a section of a number format code, insert one of the following color names in square brackets [ ]:				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3154630	29			0	uk	CYAN				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3148676	30			0	uk	GREEN				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3154123	31			0	uk	BLACK				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3149167	32			0	uk	BLUE				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3158407	33			0	uk	MAGENTA				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3149560	34			0	uk	RED				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3147502	35			0	uk	WHITE				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3153368	36			0	uk	YELLOW				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147435	111			0	uk	Conditions				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148575	235			0	uk	Conditional Brackets				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3155312	112			0	uk	You can define a number format so that it only applies when the condition that you specify is met. Conditions are enclosed by square brackets [ ].				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3159179	115			0	uk	You can use any combination of numbers and the <, <=, >, >=, = and <> operators.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3159196	236			0	uk	For example, if you want to apply different colors to different temperature data, enter:				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3150872	113			0	uk	[BLUE][<0]#,0 "°C";[RED][>30]#,0 "°C";[BLACK]#,0 "°C"				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3157870	114			0	uk	All temperatures below zero are blue, temperatures between 0 and 30 °C are black, and temperatures higher than 30 °C are red.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154833	90			0	uk	Positive and Negative Numbers				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3147295	91			0	uk	To define a number format that adds a different text to a number depending on if the number is positive, negative, or equal to zero, use the following format:				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3153727	92			0	uk	"plus" 0;"minus" 0;"null" 0				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149260	83			0	uk	Percentages and Scientific Notation				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147218	237			0	uk	Percentages				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3151168	84			0	uk	To display numbers as percentages, add the percent sign (%) to the number format.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156005	89			0	uk	Scientific Notation				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3146923	85			0	uk	Scientific notation lets you write very large numbers or very small fractions in a compact form. For example, in scientific notation, 650000 is written as 6.5 x 10^5, and 0.000065 as 6.5 x 10^-5. In \<item type=\"productname\"\>%PRODUCTNAME\</item\>, these numbers are written as 6.5E+5 and 6.5E-5, respectively. To create a number format that displays numbers using scientific notation, enter a # or 0, and then one of the following codes E-, E+, e- or e+.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	hd_id3159080	98			0	uk	Number Format Codes of Currency Formats				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3147318	99			0	uk	The default currency format for the cells in your spreadsheet is determined by the regional setting of your operating system. If you want, you can apply a custom currency symbol to a cell. For example, enter #,##0.00 € to display 4.50 € (Euros).				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3150032	167			0	uk	You can also specify the locale setting for the currency by entering the locale code for the country after the symbol. For example, [$€-407] represents Euros in Germany. To view the locale code for a country, select the country in the \<emph\>Language\</emph\> list on the \<emph\>Numbers\</emph\> tab of the \<emph\>Format Cells\</emph\> dialog.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	hd_id3157309	238			0	uk	Date and Time Formats				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153740	37			0	uk	Date Formats				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3152791	38			0	uk	To display days, months and years, use the following number format codes. 				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id610980				0	uk	Not all format codes give meaningful results for all languages.				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3152376	39			0	uk	Format				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3159130	40			0	uk	Format Code				20220827 09:49:47
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3147380	41			0	uk	Month as 3.				20220827 09:49:47
